  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-18603",
    "knowledge_point_id": "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/c4585c2a9cf0a66da02f96a17dad5986ed8c401a844276f08b367eed765c111d.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)-g(x).$",
    "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)-{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/299b996bf08e4b4e8560d013829ddd1f42751767cd3f00dcd04c23c95f2c62b4.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-1}{3-(-2)}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{3-(-2)}{3-(-2)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)-{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
